# SnapShotKit Source-Available Licence, version 1.0

Copyright 2026 Dennis van der Stelt. All rights reserved.

This is not an open source licence, and it is not trying to look like one. The source is
published so that you can read it, audit it, build it, learn from it and improve it. It is not
published so that somebody else can ship their own SnapShotKit.

Everything below is meant to be read by the person it applies to, so it is written to be read.

## 1. What you may do

You may:

a. Read the source, in whole or in part, for any purpose.
b. Build the software from source, and run what you have built.
c. Run the software, for personal or commercial purposes, subject to section 2.
d. Modify the source and run your modified version, for your own use.
e. Copy the repository on a code hosting service for the purpose of preparing a contribution
   back to the project. On GitHub this is called forking, and it is how a pull request is made.
f. Quote the source in reviews, articles, teaching material and bug reports.

## 2. What it costs

Versions before 1.0.0 may be used free of charge.

From version 1.0.0 onwards, running the software requires a licence purchased from the copyright
holder, with these exceptions, which are free of charge and perpetual:

a. Anyone whose contribution has been merged into the project.
b. Anyone the copyright holder has told in writing that they need not pay.

A purchased licence covers one person and every machine that person uses.

## 3. What you may not do

You may not:

a. Distribute, publish, sell, rent, lease, sublicense or otherwise make the software available
   to anyone else, in source or in compiled form, modified or unmodified. Building it for
   yourself is fine. Handing the result to somebody else is not.
b. Package the software for a distribution, an app store, a container image or any other
   redistribution channel.
c. Offer the software to third parties as a hosted or managed service.
d. Remove or alter any copyright, licence or attribution notice.
e. Use the name SnapShotKit, or any confusingly similar name, for a modified version.

Section 1e is the one exception to 3a: a copy on a code hosting service, made in order to
contribute, is allowed. Publishing builds from it is not.

## 4. Contributions

Contributions are welcome, and are governed by CONTRIBUTING.md in this repository. In short: you
keep the copyright in what you write, and you grant the copyright holder a licence broad enough
to ship it and to relicense the project later. That grant is what makes it possible to publish
the source at all, and a contribution cannot be merged without it.

## 5. Other people's code

The bundled Barlow and Barlow Condensed fonts are under the SIL Open Font License, which travels
with them in `src/SnapShotKit.Ui/Assets/Fonts/OFL.txt` and is unaffected by this licence.
Third-party libraries carry their own licences, which likewise are unaffected by this one.

## 6. If you break this licence

Your rights under this licence end automatically if you breach it. They come back the first time
the copyright holder says so in writing, and not before. Nothing here limits any remedy available
under copyright law.

## 7. No warranty, and no liability

The software is provided as is, without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including but
not limited to the warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and
non-infringement.

To the fullest extent permitted by law, the copyright holder is not liable for any claim, damage,
loss or other liability, whether in contract, tort or otherwise, arising from the software or
from its use. It takes screenshots. Please do not run anything important on the strength of it.

## 8. Which law

This licence is governed by the law of the Netherlands. Any dispute goes to the competent court
in the Netherlands.

